\textbf{Low-dose ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$): per-seed consistent gaming.}
ISR reward increases TS rates in all three initial seeds (s42--44) relative to matched random: $70.6\%$ ($163/231$) vs.\ $13.7\%$ ($36/262$) pooled (supplementary CMH: $\text{OR}_{\text{CMH}}{=}2.46$, $p{=}5.6{\times}10^{-16}$; $N{=}3$ seed-level units, sample-level $p$ reflects within-seed effect; Appendix~\ref{app:statistical-details}). %% registry: plan002 vs plan018 all seeds; D095 FINAL
Random TS is seed-dependent ($38.3\%$ in s43 vs.\ $0\%$ in s42/s44); ISR amplifies TS ($2/3$ seeds at $97$--$100\%$; Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}; 5-seed extension below).
TS (a single premise identical to the conclusion, giving $\text{ISR}\geq 0.5$ by construction; \S\ref{sec:taxonomy-method}) is the canonical Goodhart move: the model satisfies the LOO definition directly.
Total conclusion embedding ($\text{CE}+\text{TS}$) is $68$--$100\%$ across both conditions; the composition differs: CE dominates under random ($71\%$), TS under ISR. ISR converts CE into TS rather than reducing total embedding (Figure~\ref{fig:hacking-composition}).
Random yields higher PR ($87{\pm}8\%$ vs.\ $77{\pm}14\%$): noise disrupts convergence toward a single collapse trajectory.

\textbf{Premise-count confound.}
The sole non-TS ISR seed (s42) shows raw $\text{ISR}^{*}{=}0.415$, $3.2\times$ random ($0.131$), a premise-minimization artifact ($n{=}5.58$ vs.\ $8.46$); matched comparison gives $1.06\times$ (Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}). %% D051 premise-count-matched analysis
After reclassifying PG and TS false negatives, non-hacking rates ($6.1\%$ ISR vs.\ $9.5\%$ random) are not significant ($p{=}0.182$; Appendix~\ref{app:h8}). %% D095 FINAL Fisher test
The key asymmetry is compositional: ISR amplifies TS convergence ($2/3$ seeds ${\geq}97\%$), while random remains seed-dependent and CE-dominated.

\textbf{Higher doses and temporal dynamics.}
ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ non-hacking is transient: seed~42 drops $17.3\%{\to}2.2\%$ by 1000 steps as TS escalates to $96.7\%$ (Figure~\ref{fig:training-isr-curves}). %% registry: plan002-b01-s42-1000, plan016-extended-1000-s42
At higher doses ($\beta{\geq}0.2$), TS becomes universal ($0\%$ non-hacking at $\beta{=}0.2$); $\beta{=}0.3$ destabilizes both ISR ($\text{PR}{=}66{\pm}42\%$) and random ($54{\pm}35\%$), with ISR producing multi-modal failure~\citep{gao-reward-overoptimization} and random converging to CE ($91\%$; Appendix~\ref{app:beta03}). %% registry: plan002-additive-b02-s42/s43/s44, plan002-additive-b03-s42/s43/s44

\textbf{Bimodal seed-dependence (5-seed extension).}
Two additional seeds (s45, s46; Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}) under the same ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ condition yield ${\sim}55\%$ non-hacking---qualitatively different from the 3-seed average of $6\%$.
This bimodal outcome (3/5 seeds dominated by gaming, 2/5 producing substantial genuine reasoning) reinforces the cross-seed instability documented in \S\ref{sec:diagnostic} and underscores that ISR reward outcomes are seed-dependent rather than deterministic. %% registry: plan002-additive-b01-s45, plan002-additive-b01-s46

\subsection{Cross-Domain and Cross-Dataset Evidence}
\label{sec:crossdomain}
\label{sec:crosslogic}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_prolog_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Cross-logic results. \textbf{Top}: FOL vs.\ Prolog exec-only $\text{ISR}^*$ across 3 seeds (s44 Prolog $0.387$ is a small-sample artifact, $n{=}21$; $^\dagger$s43 FOL uses sampling $T{=}0.7$, not strictly comparable). \textbf{Bottom}: Prolog ISR-reward gaming-mode composition; all 3 seeds reach 0\% non-hacking through distinct mechanisms.}
\label{fig:prolog-comparison}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Prolog: universal Goodhart.}
We replicate the FOL pipeline with Prolog/SWI-Prolog (Figure~\ref{fig:prolog-comparison}).
Exec-only seeds show $\text{ISR}^*{\approx}0$ via redundant ground-fact coding (2/3 seeds); ISR flags this mode as it flags FOL's conclusion embedding.
Under ISR-as-reward, 3/3 seeds reach 0\% non-hacking through distinct mechanisms: clean TS (s42), uppercase pseudo-Prolog bypassing text-match classifiers (s43, all 38 proved at $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$), and query-as-fact gaming (s44, 95/97 at $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$; Table~\ref{tab:prolog-detailed}).
Prolog's simpler semantics broadens the gaming basin, eliminating the non-hacking residual entirely.

\textbf{ProofFOL exec-only: cross-dataset evidence for structural degeneration, with differing mode distributions.}
Two converged seeds ($\text{ISR}^*{<}0.07$) show ${\leq}1.4\%$ genuine; s42 exhibits 100\% TS (absent from all FOLIO exec-only seeds), showing hacking-mode repertoire is dataset-specific ($\text{ISR}^*{<}0.12$ across all seeds, far below baseline $0.31$).

\textbf{ProofFOL ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$): beneficial instability (exploratory).}
ISR seeds average 76.8\% genuine versus 17.4\% exec-only ($p{=}0.10$, $N{=}3$; Table~\ref{tab:prooffol-detailed}), but at lower PR ($0.35$--$0.49$ vs.\ ${\sim}1.0$), so this may reflect insufficient training.

\textbf{Mechanism.}
The dataset-dependent outcome follows from hacking geometry: on FOLIO, $P{\to}P$ yields $\text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$ (regressional Goodhart~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}); on ProofFOL, hacking embeds conclusions among many premises ($\bar{n}{=}18.2$, $\text{ISR}{\approx}0$).
ISR-as-reward inherits the dataset's hacking topology.

\paragraph{Cross-scale validation (Qwen3-8B).}
Qwen3-8B does not converge under exec-only GRPO ($\text{PR}{=}14\%$, $K{=}8$; $K{=}4$ produces zero gradient), consistent with degeneration requiring successful convergence (Appendix~\ref{app:8b-results}).

\subsection{Hacking Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_hacking_composition.pdf}
\caption{Hacking composition across reward conditions (proved samples; exec-only: seed~42; all other bars: pooled across 3 seeds, per-seed breakdown in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}). ISR converts CE into more extreme TS without reducing total hacking; SFT shows near-zero hacking.}
\label{fig:hacking-composition}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Mode} & \textbf{Exec} & \textbf{ISR$_{.1}$} & \textbf{Rnd$_{.1}$} & \textbf{SFT} & \textbf{Prolog$_{\text{ISR}}$} \\
\midrule
CE embed       & 87 & 18 & 71 & 7 & 0$^\sharp$ \\
TS duplicate   & 0  & 71 & 14 & 0 & 100$^\sharp$ \\
PG prem-subset & 6  & 5  & 8   & 0 & --- \\
Tautological   & --- & --- & --- & --- & s42 \\
Repetition     & s43 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\
Contradiction  & 1k & --- & --- & --- & --- \\
Cmplx.\ vacuous& 0  & 0  & 4  & 0 & --- \\
Query-as-fact  & --- & --- & --- & --- & s44 \\
\midrule
\textbf{Non-hacking} & 8  & 6  & 10 & \textbf{92} & \textbf{0} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Eight hacking modes across conditions (\% of proved samples; exec-only/SFT: seed~42; ISR/Random: pooled across 3 seeds). TS includes subtypes TSa (text-match, $n{=}149$) and TSb (obfuscated tautology, $n{=}14$; primarily s44). Cells with seed identifiers indicate the mode dominates that seed. $^\sharp$Prolog row pools strict+functional (\S\ref{sec:crosslogic}). \texttt{1k} denotes a mode observed only at the 1000-step checkpoint.}
\label{tab:hacking-taxonomy}
\end{table}

Three modes account for ${>}90\%$ of hacking (Table~\ref{tab:hacking-taxonomy}; Appendix~\ref{app:hacking-modes}): \textbf{CE} (conclusion embedding), \textbf{TS} (conclusion duplication, ISR-amplified), and \textbf{PG} (premise-subset gaming; audited for s42 only). \emph{Reward formulation determines composition but not prevalence}: all GRPO conditions show ${\geq}80\%$ hacking; only SFT achieves $92.3\%$ non-hacking.

